{# ============================================================================
   PEBBLE HERO — a colour, a cut-out, and copy down the start edge
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
       ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
       │                        ▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓                             │
       │  NEW CAMPAIGN         ▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓                            │
       │  Softness           ▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓                           │
       │  in Comfort        ▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓                          │
       │  ( Shop ▸ )       ▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓        1 / 2  ──  ‹ ›  │
       └──────────────────╴ the cut-out runs off the bottom ╶─────────┘
              ╭─────────────── the next section laps over ────────────╮

   ── What makes this hero different from an ordinary one ──
   The image is NOT a background. Each slide is a flat COLOUR with a picture
   standing in a column on it — so there is no scrim anywhere, because the copy
   never lands on the photograph and there is nothing to darken.

   `image_fit: contain` is there for a cut-out PNG, which is bottom-anchored so
   the subject runs off the lower edge. `text_tone` switches the copy between
   light and dark, because a pale ground needs dark type either way.

   Measured off the reference at 1440px: stage 750px tall, copy starting 120px
   in (the page's own measure), title ~60px on a 74px line, the CTA pill 45px
   tall, and the pager 40px off the bottom at the end edge.

   ── Why it runs up under the header ──
   The header is `position: fixed` WITH A SPACER that reserves its height, so a
   section placed after it starts below it — and a floating header card then
   sits on the page's own white with nothing behind it to be inset FROM. It
   reads as a plain full-width bar, which is exactly what it looked like the
   first time this shipped.

   `overlap_header` pulls the stage back up by that reserved height so the card
   floats on the slide's colour, the way the reference does. It is a setting and
   not automatic because only the FIRST section on a page may do this; a hero
   further down would slide under whatever is above it.

   ── The picture is the BACKGROUND, and it drifts ──
   Earlier versions of this widget gave the picture a column of the stage and a
   width setting. That was wrong for the same reason twice: the rendered result
   depended on the photograph's own aspect ratio, so one number produced a wide
   band for a landscape shot and a sliver — or a face-filling close-up — for a
   portrait one. A background has no such argument with its source.

   The drift is a transform on the <img>, not `background-attachment: fixed`,
   which iOS has never supported and which repaints the whole layer on every
   frame. The image is oversized by the travel distance in BOTH directions so no
   edge is ever exposed, and the box above it clips the difference.

   ── Cover or contain, and why the drift differs between them ──
   `cover` oversizes the picture by the travel distance in both directions, so
   the transform can move it either way without exposing an edge.

   `contain` cannot do that: the picture already fits the stage exactly, and
   growing it to make room for the drift is the one thing `contain` exists to
   prevent. So it goes the other way — the picture is INSET by the travel
   distance instead, which leaves it room to move and keeps it inside the stage
   at both extremes. It is slightly smaller than the stage as a result, and the
   slide's own colour is what shows around it.

   ── The veil ──
   The copy now lands on a photograph, so `scrim` puts a wash over the picture
   and a gradient under the words. It is per-slide because how much a slide
   needs depends on that slide's photograph, and `none` emits nothing.

   ── The lip at the bottom ──
   The section that follows laps over the stage with rounded top corners. A
   widget cannot reach the one after it, so the hero draws that lip itself: a
   band of page colour pinned to its own lower edge. `sheet_radius: 0` turns it
   off for a layout that does not want it.

   All styling lives in assets/style.css → "15.5 HERO".
   ========================================================================= #}

{% set id = widget.id %}
{% set d = widget.data %}
{% set slides = widget.blocks | default([], true) %}
{% set count = slides | length %}

{% set overlap = d.overlap_header %}
{% set overlap = true if overlap is not defined else overlap %}
{% set parallax = d.parallax %}
{% set parallax = true if parallax is not defined else parallax %}
{% set parStrength = d.parallax_strength | default(18) %}
{% set hDesk = d.height | default(750) %}
{% set hMob = d.height_mobile | default(560) %}
{% set lip = d.sheet_radius | default(0, true) %}

{% set autoplay = d.autoplay | default(true) %}
{% set speed = d.autoplay_speed | default(6) %}
{% set showArrows = d.show_arrows | default(true) %}
{% set showCounter = d.show_counter | default(true) %}

{% set isRtl = (localization.language | default('ar', true)) != 'en' %}

{# A `media` setting arrives as an object once the merchant uploads through the
   Theme Editor, but a plain URL string is what a hand-written page JSON (and
   every preview/seed value) can express. Accept both, plus `url` as well as
   `fileUrl`, so neither source silently renders nothing. #}
{% macro mediaUrl(m) %}{% if m %}{% if m is string %}{{ m }}{% elif m.fileUrl %}{{ m.fileUrl }}{% elif m.url %}{{ m.url }}{% endif %}{% endif %}{% endmacro %}


{% macro slideBody(s, isFirst, idx) %}
  {% set img = mediaUrl(s.image) | trim %}
  {% set imgM = mediaUrl(s.image_mobile) | trim %}
  {# Demo picture while the field is empty: assets/demo-*.jpg are CC0 photos
     (sources in _ref/demo-images-sources.txt). An uploaded image replaces it. #}
  {% set demoN = (idx | default(0, true)) % 2 + 1 %}
  {% set isDemo = (img | length) == 0 %}
  {% if isDemo %}
    {% set img = ('demo-hero-' ~ demoN ~ '.jpg') | assets %}
    {% set imgM = imgM if (imgM | length) else (('demo-hero-' ~ demoN ~ '-m.jpg') | assets) %}
  {% endif %}

  {# ⚠️  Test a macro's result with `| length`, never for truthiness. A macro
     returns a SafeString OBJECT, and an object is truthy even when it holds an
     empty string — `trim` and `string` both preserve safeness, so neither
     converts it back to a primitive. Guarding on the raw value instead makes
     every slide read as having an image, and an empty src renders as a broken
     box on the ones that do not. #}
  {% set hasImg = (img | length) > 0 %}
  {% set hasM = (imgM | length) > 0 %}

  {% set tone = s.text_tone | default('light', true) %}
  {% set focus = s.image_focus | default('center', true) %}
  {% set fit = s.image_fit | default('cover', true) %}
  {% set scrim = s.scrim | default('soft', true) %}
  {# The demo art is pale: light copy on it needs the strong veil until a picture is uploaded. #}
  {% if isDemo and tone == 'light' and scrim != 'none' %}{% set scrim = 'strong' %}{% endif %}

  <div class="hy-hero-slide is-{{ tone }}"
       style="--hy-slide-bg: {{ s.bg_color | default('var(--color-bg-tertiary)', true) }};">

    {% if hasImg %}
      {# The image is the slide's BACKGROUND — full bleed, behind everything.
         `data-hy-par` is the hook the parallax script moves; the element it
         translates is the <img>, never this box, because the box is what clips
         the oversize the drift needs. #}
      <div class="hy-hero-media hy-hero-media--{{ fit }}" style="--hy-hero-focus: {{ focus }};">
        {% if hasM %}
          <picture data-hy-par>
            <source media="(max-width: 767px)" srcset="{{ imgM }}"/>
            <img src="{{ img }}" alt="{{ s.title | default('', true) }}"
                 {% if isFirst %}fetchpriority="high"{% else %}loading="lazy"{% endif %}/>
          </picture>
        {% else %}
          <img src="{{ img }}" alt="{{ s.title | default('', true) }}" data-hy-par
               {% if isFirst %}fetchpriority="high"{% else %}loading="lazy"{% endif %}/>
        {% endif %}
      </div>

      {# The veil. Two layers in one element: a flat wash over the whole picture
         so nothing on it competes with the copy, and a gradient rising from the
         copy's own edge so the words have their own ground. Both scale with the
         one setting; `none` renders nothing at all. #}
      {% if scrim != 'none' %}
        <span class="hy-hero-scrim hy-hero-scrim--{{ scrim }}" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      {% endif %}
    {% endif %}

    <div class="hy-hero-content">
      <div class="hy-hero-copy">
        {% if s.eyebrow %}
          <p class="hy-hero-eyebrow">{{ s.eyebrow }}</p>
        {% endif %}

        {% if s.title %}
          {# Exactly one <h1> per page — the first slide takes it, the rest are
             <h2>. Three <h1>s in a carousel is a real SEO fault, not a nitpick. #}
          {% if isFirst %}
            <h1 class="hy-hero-title">{{ s.title }}</h1>
          {% else %}
            <h2 class="hy-hero-title">{{ s.title }}</h2>
          {% endif %}
        {% endif %}

        {% if s.description %}
          {# ⚠️  A <div>, NOT a <p>. A `text` field comes back as block HTML
             (`<p>…</p>`), and a <p> cannot contain a <p> — the browser closes
             the outer one and the content becomes a SIBLING, so every style on
             this class stops reaching the text. Inner margins are reset in the
             CSS. #}
          <div class="hy-hero-desc">{{ s.description | safe }}</div>
        {% endif %}

        {% if s.cta_text %}
          {% set style = s.cta_style | default('light', true) %}
          <a href="{{ s.cta_url | default('/shop', true) }}"
             class="hy-btn hy-btn--lg hy-btn--pill hy-btn--arrow hy-btn--{{ style }}">
            <span>{{ s.cta_text }}</span>
            <i class="hy-btn-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg class="rtl-flip" width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                   stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round"
                   stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m9 6 6 6-6 6"/></svg>
            </i>
          </a>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endmacro %}


{# No title to lead with: the store name is the page's H1, outside the section because `data-hy-empty` hides it. #}
{% set firstTitle = slides[0].data.title if count > 0 else '' %}
{% if not firstTitle and store.name %}<h1 class="hy-sr-only">{{ store.name }}</h1>{% endif %}
<section id="hy-hero-{{ id }}"{% if count == 0 %} data-hy-empty{% endif %} class="hy-hero {{ 'is-overlapping' if overlap }}">
  <style>
    #hy-hero-{{ id }} {
      --hy-hero-h: {{ hMob }}px;
      --hy-hero-lip: {{ lip }}px;
      --hy-hero-par: {{ parStrength }};
    }
    @media (min-width: 768px) {
      #hy-hero-{{ id }} { --hy-hero-h: {{ hDesk }}px; }
    }
  </style>

  <div id="hy-heroStage-{{ id }}" class="hy-hero-stage">

    {% if count > 1 %}
      {# Swiper resolves direction off the element (`el.dir`, then the computed
         style) — there is no `rtl` init option. The fade effect does not
         translate, so this changes nothing visually here; it keeps every slider
         in the theme declaring direction the same way. #}
      <div id="hy-heroSw-{{ id }}" class="swiper" dir="{{ 'rtl' if isRtl else 'ltr' }}">
        <div class="swiper-wrapper">
          {% for block in slides %}
            <div class="swiper-slide">
              {{ slideBody(block.data, loop.first, loop.index0) }}
            </div>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
      </div>
    {% elif count == 1 %}
      {{ slideBody(slides[0].data, true, 0) }}
    {% else %}
      {# No slides yet. The stage is drawn at its real height with the copy
         block sketched in, so the merchant sees the shape they are filling
         rather than a collapsed strip. A preset cannot seed blocks — see the
         note in style.css → "15.29.1". #}
      <div class="hy-hero-slide is-light" data-hy-placeholder aria-hidden="true"
           style="--hy-slide-bg: var(--color-bg-tertiary);">
        <div class="hy-hero-content">
          <div class="hy-hero-copy">
            <span class="hy-ph-line hy-ph-line--sm" style="--ph-line-w: 30%;"></span>
            <span class="hy-ph-line hy-ph-line--lg"></span>
            <span class="hy-ph-line hy-ph-line--lg" style="--ph-line-w: 62%;"></span>
            <span class="hy-ph-hint" style="margin-block-start: 14px;">
              <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/>
              </svg>
              {{ t('common.placeholder_add') }}
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    {% endif %}

    {% if count > 1 and (showArrows or showCounter) %}
      <div class="hy-hero-pager">
        {% if showCounter %}
          {# The numbers are written by Swiper's own pagination in `custom`
             render mode, and the bar underneath is a plain element whose width
             this widget's script sets. Swiper's `progressbar` type would have
             been one element for both jobs — and it cannot carry the numbers. #}
          <span class="hy-hero-count">
            <span id="hy-heroNow-{{ id }}">1</span>
            <span class="hy-hero-count-sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
            <span>{{ count }}</span>
          </span>
          <span class="hy-hero-track">
            <span id="hy-heroBar-{{ id }}" class="hy-hero-bar"></span>
          </span>
        {% endif %}

        {% if showArrows %}
          <span class="hy-hero-arrows">
            <button type="button" id="hy-heroPrev-{{ id }}" class="hy-hero-arrow" aria-label="{{ t('common.prev') }}">
              <svg class="rtl-flip" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" aria-hidden="true">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M15 6 9 12l6 6"/>
              </svg>
            </button>
            <button type="button" id="hy-heroNext-{{ id }}" class="hy-hero-arrow" aria-label="{{ t('common.next') }}">
              <svg class="rtl-flip" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" aria-hidden="true">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="m9 6 6 6-6 6"/>
              </svg>
            </button>
          </span>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    {% endif %}

    {# The lip the next section would draw if a widget could reach it. Last in
       the stage so it paints over the slide, and `aria-hidden` because it is
       pure decoration standing in for a neighbour. #}
    {% if lip > 0 %}
      <span class="hy-hero-lip" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    {% endif %}

  </div>
</section>

<script>
  (function () {
    var stage = document.getElementById('hy-heroStage-{{ id }}');
    if (!stage) return;

    {% if parallax %}
    /* ---- Parallax ----
       A transform on the <img>, not `background-attachment: fixed` — iOS has
       never supported that, and where it does work it repaints the whole layer
       on every frame.

       The picture is oversized by the travel distance in BOTH directions and
       the box above it clips the difference, so no edge is ever exposed no
       matter where the stage sits in the viewport.

       Position is read from the STAGE's rect rather than from scrollY, so the
       effect is the same whether this hero is the first section on the page or
       the fifth. The sign is what makes it read as lag: scrolling down moves
       the rect up, `top` goes negative, the shift goes positive, and the
       picture slides DOWN against the page. */
    (function () {
      var reduce = document.documentElement.classList.contains('hy-no-motion') || (window.matchMedia
        && window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches);
      if (reduce) return;

      var layers = stage.querySelectorAll('[data-hy-par]');
      if (!layers.length) return;

      var factor = {{ parStrength }} / 100;
      var travel = 0;
      var ticking = false;

      /* Remeasured on resize AND after the fonts settle: the stage's height is
         a `min-height` the copy can exceed, so it is not known until layout. */
      function measure() {
        var h = stage.offsetHeight || 0;
        var vh = window.innerHeight || 0;
        travel = (vh + h) / 2 * factor;
        stage.style.setProperty('--hy-par-px', travel.toFixed(1) + 'px');
      }

      function apply() {
        ticking = false;
        var r = stage.getBoundingClientRect();
        var vh = window.innerHeight || 0;
        /* 0 when the stage's centre is on the viewport's centre; ±travel at the
           two extremes where it is entirely off one edge. */
        var shift = (vh / 2 - (r.top + r.height / 2)) * factor;
        if (shift > travel) shift = travel;
        if (shift < -travel) shift = -travel;
        for (var i = 0; i < layers.length; i++) {
          layers[i].style.transform = 'translate3d(0,' + shift.toFixed(1) + 'px,0)';
        }
      }

      function onScroll() {
        if (ticking) return;
        ticking = true;
        window.requestAnimationFrame(apply);
      }

      measure(); apply();
      window.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, { passive: true });
      window.addEventListener('resize', function () { measure(); apply(); });
      window.addEventListener('load', function () { measure(); apply(); });
      if (document.fonts && document.fonts.ready) {
        document.fonts.ready.then(function () { measure(); apply(); });
      }
    })();
    {% endif %}

    if ({{ count }} < 2) return;

    var el = document.getElementById('hy-heroSw-{{ id }}');
    if (!el || typeof Swiper === 'undefined') return;

    var now = document.getElementById('hy-heroNow-{{ id }}');
    var bar = document.getElementById('hy-heroBar-{{ id }}');
    var total = {{ count }};

    /* The counter and the bar are the same fact twice, so they are written
       together. `realIndex`, not `activeIndex`: with `loop: true` Swiper keeps
       duplicated slides at both ends and `activeIndex` counts those — the
       counter read "3 / 2" on the last slide before this was corrected. */
    function sync(sw) {
      var i = sw.realIndex + 1;
      if (now) now.textContent = i;
      if (bar) bar.style.width = (i / total * 100) + '%';
    }

    new Swiper(el, {
      /* Crossfade, measured off the reference: the background colour blends
         through an intermediate value between the two slides rather than one
         sliding over the other. */
      effect: 'fade',
      fadeEffect: { crossFade: true },
      speed: 600,
      loop: true,
      grabCursor: false,
      {% if autoplay and settings.enableAnimations != false %}
      autoplay: { delay: {{ speed * 1000 }}, disableOnInteraction: false, pauseOnMouseEnter: true },
      {% endif %}
      {% if showArrows %}
      navigation: { prevEl: '#hy-heroPrev-{{ id }}', nextEl: '#hy-heroNext-{{ id }}' },
      {% endif %}
      a11y: { enabled: true },
      on: {
        init: function () { sync(this); },
        slideChange: function () { sync(this); }
      }
    });
  })();
</script>

{# Per-section palette. Emits nothing unless `colors_enabled` is on, so the
   switch IS the revert. Must stay after the widget's own <style> — see the
   partial's header for why. #}
{% ui "section-colors.njk", scope='#hy-hero-' ~ id, c=d %}
